# Advanced Microeconomics

**Authors:** Bibhas Saha

## Abstract
The aim of this course is to give students a solid foundation of formal microeconomic modeling. For this reason we will focus on standard microeconomic topics starting from consumer and producer theory, and partial equilibrium analysis. We will also cover in details choice under uncertainty and some modern developments in comparative statics. With any luck we should be able to cover also principal-agent models with hidden actions and information. If time allows we can also cover some other topics of students interest including: adverse selection, externalities & public goods. Recall that other important microeconomic topics are covered in my separate lectures: general equilibrium (233460-0089), industrial organization (230250-0089), game theory (222940-0089) or welfare economics (233190). Our basic textbook is Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green (1995), but (some new) detailed readings of topics covered will be provided in class. The course aims to be formal hence students are expected to be at least not afraid of basic mathematical tools including: calculus, (smooth) constrained optimization, convex analysis or linear algebra. Knowledge of mathematical appendix of Mas-Colell, Whinston, and Green (1995) should suffice. Mid term exam (25%), final exam (25%), homework (50%).

